%I A398453 #19 Aug 06 2026 22:42:14
%S A398453 0,1,2,3,8,9,10,20,21,30,31,80,88,90,100,200,210,300,310,800,880,900,
%T A398453 1000,2000,2100,3000,3100,8000,8800,9000,10000,20000,21000,30000,
%U A398453 31000,80000,88000,90000,100000,200000,210000,300000,310000,800000,880000,900000,1000000,2000000
%N A398453 Integers m such that digits in m and m^2 are in nonincreasing order.
%C A398453 Similar to A234841 where digits are in nondecreasing order.
%C A398453 In Diophante link, these numbers are called "nombres monotones".
%C A398453 If m is a term, so is 10*m, since tail of zeros are in nonincreasing order.
%C A398453 {0, 1, 2, 3, 8, 9, 21, 31, 88} is the set of primitive terms, then all terms are obtained by multiplying these primitive integers by 10^k, k >= 0.
%H A398453 Diophante, <a href="http://www.diophante.fr/problemes-par-themes/arithmetique-et-algebre/a3-nombres-remarquables/203-a318-les-nombres-monotones">A318 = Les nombres monotones</a> (in French).
%e A398453 31 and 31^2 = 961 have digits in nonincreasing order, so 31 is a term.
%e A398453 880 and 880^2 = 774400 have digits in nonincreasing order, so 880 is another term.
%t A398453 q[m_] := And @@ (GreaterEqual @@ IntegerDigits[#] & /@ {m, m^2}); Select[Range[0, 2000000], q] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Jul 30 2026 *)
%Y A398453 Intersection of A009996 and A028821.
%Y A398453 Subsequences: A011557, A052268, A093136, A093138, A346178.
%Y A398453 Cf. A234841.
%K A398453 nonn,base,easy
%O A398453 1,3
%A A398453 _Bernard Schott_, Jul 30 2026
